  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...No significant changes to forecast thinking as a\nconsolidation of surface lows is expected to occur along an \nelongated baroclinic zone off the U.S. East Coast later today. \nRapid deepening of this low pressure is then forecast later \ntonight into Thursday as a notable coastal low takes shape. This\nlow is then forecast to move north towards Southern New \nEngland, then eventually weaken as it potentially wobbles around\nthe New England coast over the weekend. Regarding the track and\nthe strength of this low, most guidance has shifted slightly \nlater in this lows exit keeping stronger winds in place \nslightly longer across the area waters and OBX. Will note, not \nall guidance is as slow or as deep with this lows departure and\nwhile the current forecast has trended slightly up compared to \nthe previous forecast, some further adjustments including a \ndecrease in wind potential is not out of the question just yet.\n\nRegarding sensible weather, scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms are expected to persist through Thursday with the \nbest chances to see precipitation likely along and east of Hwy \n17. While an area of focused lift and higher rainfall totals may\nstill develop, the latest guidance continues to back off on \nthis potential, and it appears the risk of flooding due to rain \nremains low.\n\nBy far, the greatest impacts with this coastal low are expected\nfor the Outer Banks. There, a period of moderate to strong \nnortheast to north winds are expected to develop later today, \nand then potentially last well into the day Friday and \npotentially as late as Saturday morning, especially if the \nlatest westward and slower trend holds. Probabilistic guidance \nshows a strong signal for reaching Wind Advisory criteria with \nboth sustained winds and gusts, and the wind advisory for Dare \nCounty, Ocracoke, and Downeast Carteret County remains in effect\nstarting later this afternoon. For the OBX from Cape Hatteras \nnorth, peak gusts could top out around 50 mph, and this may lead\nto some minor tree and power line damage, along with a risk of \nisolated power outages.\n\nThe strong, and longer duration, of the winds is also expected \nto support an increased risk of coastal impacts. Building seas \nand increasing swell periods will lead to dangerous surf \nconditions in addition to a risk of beach erosion, wave runup, \nocean overwash, and minor to moderate coastal flooding. Wave \nheights in the surf zone may reach 10-15ft at 10-13s from Duck \nto Cape Hatteras, peaking on Thursday. 6-8ft+ wave heights in \nthe surf zone may last into this weekend. A High Surf Advisory \nRemains in effect through Saturday evening. The strength, and \nduration, of the swell is expected to lead to an increased risk \nof ocean overwash and moderate coastal flooding for the \nvulnerable stretches of the coastline along Hatteras Island. \nWhere dune structures are less vulnerable, minor coastal \nflooding will be possible.\n\nFor soundside areas adjacent to the southern Pamlico Sound, and\nthe nearby Neuse River, persistent northeasterly winds may lead\nto minor coastal flooding later Wednesday through Friday.\nEventually the winds are expected to turn more northerly, which\nmay then allow the soundside coastal flooding risk to shift to\nportions of Ocracoke and Hatteras Villages.\n\nPlease continue to stay tuned for future updates as this is a\ncomplex and longer-duration coastal low event, with subtle\nchanges in strength and path of the low leading to increased, or\ndecreased, coastal impacts.",
